1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,600
Welcome to Two-Minute Training Tuesday.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,700
This is an example export file.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,800
Three hundred and eighty lines.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,400
You need a unique list of the vendors we actually bought from.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,300
Instead of copy, paste, Remove Duplicates

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,100
Here's a better way.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,500
One formula: UNIQUE — and the vendor column.

8
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,900
Excel writes the table reference for you.

9
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:35,900
Fifteen vendors, out of three hundred and eighty lines.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,400
I never told it fifteen. It took exactly the space it needed.

11
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,400
Every cell in that list belongs to the one formula in B5.

12
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,200
That's a spill range.

13
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:54,500
It isn't sorted — so wrap the whole thing in SORT.

14
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,900
One more function around the outside. Nothing else changes.

15
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:03,500
Alphabetical. Still one formula.

16
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,700
Now the most powerful one.

17
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:13,000
FILTER — give it the whole table...

18
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:20,900
...then the rule: cost center equals this cell.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,500
A hundred and thirty-six lines — your whole report, sorted by that one filter.

20
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,900
Similar to a pivot table. And here's the part that matters.

21
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,500
Change the cost center...

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,500
...and the whole report redraws itself.

23
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,500
No copying. No pasting. No re-sorting.

24
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:49,100
Next week: how we built that drop-down.
